title open problem 4: a report that reads the same run either way

# Section 15, open problem 4.  This one is presentation, and the
# document has already made its choice: reports name onto and off-of,
# never left and right, so a component reads identically whichever
# direction the rebase runs.
#
# The example is a pair of ordinary conflicts, chosen because both are
# symmetric in kind:
#
#   journal.txt  one side deletes it, the other edits it -- lineage.
#   config.ini   both sides write different bytes -- content.
#   quiet.txt    nobody touched it, and it decides.
#
# The claim to test is not about this document alone but about the
# document with its onto and off-of blocks exchanged: same conflicts,
# same certificates with the two sides swapped, and no report anywhere
# that says "left" or "right".  Swap the two boxes on the page and
# read the cards; examples-check.js does the same swap and asserts it.
#
# One thing that is NOT symmetric, and should not be: the bytes that
# come out.  Onto is the substrate, so a quarantined region keeps
# whatever onto had.  Run it as written and config.ini comes out
# config-onto; run it swapped and the same file comes out
# config-offof.  The disagreement reads identically from either end;
# the result cannot, because a rebase has a direction.  Open problem 4
# is about the report, and only about the report.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: journal.txt = journal-v1
    3-100: config.ini = config-v1
    4-100: quiet.txt = quiet-v1

tree onto
  1-1: /
    # journal.txt deleted.
    3-100: config.ini = config-onto
    4-100: quiet.txt = quiet-v1

tree offof
  1-1: /
    2-100: journal.txt = journal-v2
    3-100: config.ini = config-offof
    4-100: quiet.txt = quiet-v1

# --- expected result, generated by gen-expected.js ---
expect conflict content
expect conflict lineage
expect quarantined /config.ini

tree expected
  1-1: /
    3-100: config.ini = config-onto
    4-100: quiet.txt = quiet-v1
